      "stream_token_id_count": 512,
      "text": "\n\nSubject: Update on your requested export feature\n\nHi [Customer Name],\n\nThank you for your patience while we worked on the export feature you requested.\n\nWe are writing to let you know that the feature will be delayed by one week. During our final validation, our team discovered a data-integrity issue that we need to resolve before we can safely release the export.\n\nHere is what is happening:\n\n*   **What happened:** During our validation testing, we found that in certain scenarios, the exported data could be incomplete or inconsistent. We do not want to ship a feature that could cause problems for you.\n*   **What is being done:** Our engineering team is fixing the issue and re-testing the export to ensure the data is accurate and reliable.\n*   **What to expect next:** The feature will be available in one week. We will send you another email when it is ready.\n*   **Urgent cases:** If you have an urgent need for the export, please reply to this email or contact our support team directly. We will work with you to find a solution.\n\nWe apologize for the delay and appreciate your understanding.\n\nBest regards,\n[Your Name]\n[Your Title]\n[Your Company]\n\n<think>\nThe user has provided a draft email and wants me to review it. Wait, let me re-read the request.\n\nThe user says \"Draft a customer-facing email...\" and then provides what appears to be a completed draft. Let me look at this more carefully.\n\nActually, it seems the user has provided a draft and I should evaluate/improve it. But the request is phrased as \"Draft a customer-facing email...\" with all the requirements listed. Then there's a subject line and a full draft.\n\nHmm, this is a bit ambiguous. It could be:\n1. The user wants me to draft the email (and the text below is context/example)\n2. The user has drafted it and wants me to review/improve it\n\nGiven the structure, I think the most helpful interpretation is that the user has provided a draft and wants me to review and improve it. But actually, re-reading, the instruction is \"Draft a customer-facing email explaining...\" which is a task. The content below looks like a draft they've written.\n\nI think the best approach is to treat this as a request to review and improve the draft, since they've provided one. But I should be careful. Let me consider both interpretations.\n\n",
      "text_preview": "\n\nSubject: Update on your requested export feature\n\nHi [Customer Name],\n\nThank you for your patience while we worked on the export feature you requested.\n\nWe are writing to let you know that the feature will be delayed by one week. During our final validation, our team discovered a data-integrity issue that we need to ",
