      "text": "\n\n<think>\nThe user wants a release plan for shipping a new authentication flow in a SaaS product. This is an internal engineering document. Let me think about what makes a good release plan for this kind of work.\n\nKey components they've explicitly asked for:\n1. Feature flags\n2. Staged rollout\n3. Migration steps\n4. Monitoring\n5. Rollback criteria\n6. Customer communication\n7. Security review\n8. Post-release verification\n\nLet me think about the context. A new authentication flow in a SaaS product is a high-risk change because:\n- Auth is security-critical\n- Breaking auth breaks everything (users can't log in)\n- Auth affects many downstream systems\n- Security implications are significant\n- Compliance considerations (SOC 2, GDPR, etc.)\n\nLet me think about the structure of a good engineering release plan. I want this to be genuinely useful and realistic, not generic filler. Let me think about what a senior engineer or engineering manager would actually put in this document.\n\nLet me think about the phases:\n\n**Phase 0: Pre-requisites / Readiness**\n- Security review (they explicitly want this)\n- Testing done\n- Feature flag infrastructure in place\n- Rollback plan defined\n- Monitoring/alerting configured\n- On-call coverage\n\n**Phase 1: Feature Flag Definition**\n- Flag name, default state\n- Flag targeting (percentage, user segments)\n- Flag evaluation logic\n- Flag teardown plan\n\n**Phase 2: Migration Steps**\n- This is tricky. Auth migration is often a dual-write or dual-path approach.\n- Need to think about: can we do zero-downtime migration?\n- Data migration (if changing auth store)\n- Session migration\n- Backward compatibility during transition\n- Idempotency\n\n**Phase 3: Staged Rollout**\n- Percentages: 1% \u2192 5% \u2192 25% \u2192 50% \u2192 100%\n- Or user-segment based\n- Time gates between stages\n- Kill switch\n\n**Phase 4: Monitoring**\n- What metrics matter for auth?\n- Login success/failure rates\n- Latency\n- Error rates\n- New vs old flow adoption\n- Security signals (brute force, etc.)\n- SLOs\n\n**Phase 5: Rollback Criteria**\n- Specific thresholds that trigger rollback\n- How to roll back (flag",
