      "request_ended_epoch_s": 1787873855.5818942,
      "request_id": "bench-qwen36-27b-autoround-int4-b70-realistic-v1-10-performance-hypotheses",
      "request_started_epoch_s": 1787873845.052427,
      "response_id_first": null,
      "response_id_last": null,
      "response_x_request_id": null,
      "sha256": "b60f6cb124e7b904139b5e67e5a49239f4a66b6ce47fe6fefb7efc193d9f1e50",
      "stream_token_id_count": 512,
      "text": "\n\n<think>\nThe user is asking about GPU inference kernel profiling for a Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) model, specifically during the decode phase. They want performance hypotheses to test, covering several specific areas:\n\n1. Kernel fusion\n2. Quantized matmul layout\n3. Route caching\n4. Graph capture\n5. Batching shape\n6. Memory bandwidth\n7. Synchronization overhead\n8. Validation traps\n\nLet me think deeply about MoE inference during decode, and what dominates the time.\n\n**Background on MoE decode:**\n\nIn MoE models (like Mixtral, GPT-4, DeepSeek, etc.), each token is routed to a subset of experts based on a gating network. During decode (autoregressive generation), we process one token at a time (batch size 1 for the sequence dimension, though there can be batched decoding).\n\nThe MoE forward pass involves:\n1. **Gate/up/down projections**: The gate network takes the token embedding and produces routing scores. Then these scores are used to select experts. The \"up\" and \"down\" projections are the expert projections.\n\nWait, let me clarify the terminology. In MoE:\n- **Gate projection**: Maps token embedding \u2192 gating logits \u2192 softmax \u2192 router weights\n- **Up/down projections**: These are the expert projections. Each expert has an up-projection and down-projection (like a 2-layer MLP per expert). The \"up\" maps input to a hidden dimension, and \"down\" maps back.\n\nActually, in the context of MoE, the structure per expert is typically:\n- Input \u2192 up-projection (linear) \u2192 hidden \u2192 down-projection (linear) \u2192 output\n\nSo the \"gate/up/down projections dominate decode time\" means:\n- Gate projection: routing computation\n- Up projections: the up-matmuls for all selected experts\n- Down projections: the down-matmuls for all selected experts\n\nDuring decode, the batch size is 1 (single token), so we're doing matmuls with a single row vector. This is a key characteristic.\n\n**Why decode is slow:**\n\n1. **Small batch size (1)**: Matmuls with batch=1 are inefficient on GPUs. GPUs want large batch sizes to saturate the tensor cores and hide latency. A single token means the matmul is essentially a matrix-vector product (GEMV), which has poor arithmetic intensity and can't use tensor cores efficiently",
      "text_preview": "\n\n<think>\nThe user is asking about GPU inference kernel profiling for a Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) model, specifically during the decode phase. They want performance hypotheses to test, covering several specific areas:\n\n1. Kernel fusion\n2. Quantized matmul layout\n3. Route caching\n4. Graph capture\n5. Batching shape\n6. Mem",
