      "text": "\n\n<think>\nThe user wants a decision memo about whether to keep optimizing the current model or switch to a different model family. This is a classic decision memo format used in ML/AI engineering teams. Let me think about what makes a good decision memo.\n\nA decision memo (also called a decision record) is a structured document that captures:\n1. The decision being made\n2. Context/background\n3. Evidence\n4. Alternatives considered\n5. The decision itself\n6. Rationale\n7. Consequences/next steps\n8. What to preserve\n\nThe user has specifically asked for these elements:\n- Current evidence\n- Opportunity cost\n- Criteria for stopping\n- Expected next experiments\n- Quality constraints\n- What artifacts should be preserved for future work\n\nLet me think about the format. A decision memo should be:\n- Concise but complete\n- Decision-focused (state the decision clearly up front)\n- Evidence-based\n- Include the reasoning\n- Actionable next steps\n\nI should probably present this as a template/example that's realistic and useful. Since the user hasn't given me specific data, I'll create a realistic scenario with plausible numbers and evidence, but I should make it clear this is a template/example they can adapt. Actually, let me think about whether they want a specific scenario or a template.\n\nGiven the phrasing \"whether to keep optimizing the current model or switch to a different model family,\" this is a general decision. I think the best approach is to write a decision memo that's realistic and complete, using plausible placeholder-ish data, but structured so it's clearly a decision memo they can use. I'll make it concrete with example numbers so it reads as a real memo, but I could note that they should fill in their actual data.\n\nLet me think about the structure of a good decision memo:\n\n**Header**\n- Title\n- Date\n- Decision owner\n- Status (DECIDED / PROPOSED)\n\n**1. Decision**\n- Clear statement of the decision\n\n**2. Context / Background**\n- Why this decision matters\n- Current state\n\n**3. Evidence**\n- Current evidence for keeping optimizing\n- Current evidence for switching\n- Data points, metrics\n\n**4. Alternatives**\n- Option A: Keep optimizing current model\n- Option B: Switch to different model family\n- Maybe Option C: hold/other\n\n**5. Opportunity Cost**\n- What we give up with each option",
      "text_preview": "\n\n<think>\nThe user wants a decision memo about whether to keep optimizing the current model or switch to a different model family. This is a classic decision memo format used in ML/AI engineering teams. Let me think about what makes a good decision memo.\n\nA decision memo (also called a decision record) is a structured ",
